WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:02.290
- [Mark] Okay, Maggie.

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:03.860
Maggie, where you originally?

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.010
- Northern Virginia,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.170
just outside of Washington, DC.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.180
- [Mark] And, what brought
you out here to skid row?

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:11.013
- [Maggie] Well, what brought me

00:00:11.013 --> 00:00:13.160
to Los Angeles was treatment, originally.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.698
And that was in 2015.

00:00:14.698 --> 00:00:16.338
- [Mark] Which drug?

00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:18.423
- At that time it was alcohol.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.010
I've been using drugs
since I was, I mean, well,

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.830
if you include weed and alcohol, like 15.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:27.963
But, I've been using heroin
on and off since I was 18.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.210
I was able to put it
down for about 12 years

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.113
but I became a raging
alcoholic and crack smoker.

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:40.950
So then in 2014 I was in a
horrific motorcycle accident.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.230
And a man with cataracts
hit me from behind,

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.110
the irony of this story

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.030
is that I'd offered, 60 days sober,

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:48.690
got offered to go to the bar that night

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.240
and I chose to go home.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.320
And I got hit and thrown
from my motorcycle

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.420
and opened my eyes when
I laying on the ground.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.630
And the car's still
coming straight for me.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.894
If I had just gotten knocked off my bike,

00:00:58.894 --> 00:01:00.920
I would had a broken
wrist and some scrapes.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.998
But, I'm lying there and the
car literally runs me over

00:01:02.998 --> 00:01:05.010
with its front and rear passenger tires,

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.110
like I'm a speed bump.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.420
Luckily I'm blessed because
it shattered my pelvis.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.140
But, a little bit lower,
I would've been paralyzed,

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:12.973
and a little but higher
I would have been dead.

00:01:12.973 --> 00:01:15.110
I mean, you can't even
tell looking at me today.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:16.852
- [Mark] No, I would never suspect.

00:01:16.852 --> 00:01:18.960
- And my pelvis was shattered.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.990
Only 9% of the time when
the bones break in that way,

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:22.250
if you don't die on impact,

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.840
they rupture an internal organ

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:24.730
and that didn't happen either.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.020
So, that was in 2014.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.500
And 2015, like, right after that,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.470
like, as soon as I got to the hospital,

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.350
I was like give me a
bottle, like fuck this.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:33.220
Like, forget it.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.320
Like, forget sobriety.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:36.810
And I was on all kinds of opiates.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.440
I mean, they had the
button, they had everything.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.740
I mean, literally, my pelvis was shattered

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.590
and that's the term in the ER report.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.130
And, at this point, I was
actually a professional

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.470
Broadway jazz dancer.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:49.970
I danced in the company of Chicago.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.270
I've danced with Lion King

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.163
and my pelvis didn't heal correctly.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.480
So, now I've got one leg
that's shorter than the other.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.570
And dancers, if their hips aren't aligned,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.910
basically their career's done.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:06.883
So, that was also another
major, you know, blow.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:08.700
And, like I said, at this point

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.700
I hadn't done heroin in
about 9 and a half, 10 years.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.740
And I didn't abuse the
prescription medication,

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.590
but I drank like a fish.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:24.590
And so I came out here to
Palm Springs for treatment.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.428
And then I got sent to an IOP
in a sober living out here.

00:02:28.428 --> 00:02:30.328
And I was sober for a year and a half.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.200
And after that year and a half

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.350
I'd had a botched surgery at John Hopkins.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:36.470
They told me my pelvis was healed,

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.260
they took out all the
hardware and they were wrong.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.640
My pelvis broke away, the right side,

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.028
broke away from the base of my spine.

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:45.600
And they took out the bolt, if you will,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.700
the screw, that was holding that together.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:49.723
So, for a while

00:02:49.723 --> 00:02:51.180
my right leg was not attached
to the rest of my body

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.500
and I had to have a fusion
at the base of my spine.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.860
And the recovery from that
surgery was hellacious.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.330
I had extreme nerve damage,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:01.500
I had a pool of blood dry under incision.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.442
And then I was having
excruciating nerve pain

00:03:04.442 --> 00:03:05.660
going down my leg.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:06.493
And there's, I mean,

00:03:06.493 --> 00:03:08.050
no amount of opiates will touch that pain.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.840
There's really nothing
other than Gabapentin

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.860
and maybe Lyrica that touches nerve pain.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.030
But, this was so excruciating

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.560
and the surgeon was like,
"There's nothing wrong.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:16.470
There's nothing wrong."

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.710
You know, "It's just nerve pain,"

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:19.270
head of ortho at Cedars.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:24.143
And I relapsed after a
year and a half sober.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.220
My boyfriend was moving all of his stuff

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.700
out of our apartment while I
was having the procedure done.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.090
And they found a softball size mass

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.480
on my pelvis that no
one had told me about.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.160
You know, I never got angry at God

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:38.993
when the accident happened.

00:03:38.993 --> 00:03:42.500
I wasn't, literally, before
the second tire went over me,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.460
I said, "Okay, God, here we go."

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.840
And not, you know, like, "All
right, I'm going to die,"

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.210
just like, "You're with
me, no matter what."

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:53.360
And at this point I was angry.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.240
I was distraught.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.660
Why did you save my life
to make it this hell?

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.330
Why? Like, what?

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.440
You know, I gave glory to
God and profess your name.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.850
Like, why?

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:04.683
Why?

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.730
And so I relapsed.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:09.350
I Googled, literally,

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.400
Googled on where to find
drugs in Los Angeles,

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.233
'cause I hadn't used here.

00:04:12.233 --> 00:04:14.193
And it sent me to MacArthur park.

00:04:15.135 --> 00:04:15.968
- [Mark] Really?

00:04:15.968 --> 00:04:16.801
- Yeah.
(both laugh)

00:04:16.801 --> 00:04:17.768
Yeah, it's cleaned up a little bit now.

00:04:17.768 --> 00:04:18.630
(Maggie drowns out Mark)

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.940
Yeah.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:22.090
Anyway, well, that's where I went.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:23.610
It's a lot cleaner now than it was,

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:24.853
this was in 2017,

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.780
about February, 2017.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:33.780
And, you know, I relapsed on heroin.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.230
My drugs of choice are
alcohol, heroin and crack.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.360
And I just I threw it all away,

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.460
I was so emotionally broken at that point.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:45.766
And, so

00:04:45.766 --> 00:04:48.190
(Maggie clears her throat)

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:50.523
since that surgery in 2017,

00:04:51.865 --> 00:04:54.210
and the fact that I was already an addict,

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.150
coupled with the fact that I
got a large amount of money.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:57.459
Because the accident was my fault.

00:04:57.459 --> 00:04:58.659
- [Mark] I was going to ask you that, but-

00:04:58.659 --> 00:04:59.492
- Yeah.

00:04:59.492 --> 00:05:00.503
- [Mark] You might get some settle-

00:05:00.503 --> 00:05:01.336
- Yeah.

00:05:01.336 --> 00:05:02.169
And

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:03.943
so

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:06.627
I relapsed

00:05:06.627 --> 00:05:10.070
and I have not been able to get it back

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:11.103
since 2017.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:13.860
In the past I've had-

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.110
My first time in treatment
was when I was 18.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:21.080
The third time I went to
treatment at 20 years old,

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.253
I got two years sober.

00:05:23.645 --> 00:05:27.130
And then I've had like nine months

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:28.480
and then that year and a half.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.383
But, since that surgery,

00:05:30.383 --> 00:05:32.310
I just haven't been able to get it back.

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.143
Got really bad chronic pain.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.900
And, you know, if we want
to do statistic wise,

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.803
like, even lower for
people with chronic pain.

00:05:42.429 --> 00:05:43.530
I'm not saying that it can't be done,

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:44.810
because there are people that do it.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:48.330
And I got sober.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:49.680
I ended up down in skid row

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.870
about two years ago for the first time.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.540
And, like I said, I had a lot of money,

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.650
I didn't have to sell myself for drugs,

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:03.370
I didn't have to-

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.720
Robbing, stealing's never
really been my thing.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.960
If I need to, I'll prostitute myself

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.150
because I can accept
responsibility for that.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.411
And I'm also not going
to have someone else

00:06:13.411 --> 00:06:14.530
try and control me.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:16.150
You know, there's guys that want to, like,

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:17.130
dangle drugs in front of you

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:21.690
and I'd much rather go
out and make it myself

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:25.080
and compromise myself rather
than compromise anyone else.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:25.913
I don't know if-

00:06:25.913 --> 00:06:28.183
I feel like there's some
odd integrity in that.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.153
Well, for whatever that's worth

00:06:31.153 --> 00:06:32.463
if that makes any sense to anybody.

00:06:35.731 --> 00:06:39.000
Sorry, I'm kind of jumping
all over the place.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.310
So, I ended up living
down here for three months

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:45.210
and I met a girl that I
call my skid row angel.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.273
She was in a situation
where she had been beaten

00:06:47.273 --> 00:06:48.780
and needed to get out of it.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.250
And I just did the next right thing

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.285
and helped someone, you
know, have a place to stay.

00:06:52.285 --> 00:06:53.710
I said, you can stay in this hotel room,

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:55.530
pay that guest for you if you need to.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.070
And, I don't think I'd be alive today,

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:58.533
down here, if it wasn't for her.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.893
And, you know, I really
got blessed in that way.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:05.440
But, I stayed down here for three months-

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.042
- [Mark] By helping her, it helped you.

00:07:07.042 --> 00:07:08.030
- Basically.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:09.880
And I just did the next right thing.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:10.713
And

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:14.300
then I went to treatment.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:15.340
I mean, I've been in and out of treatment

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:17.040
for the last two and a half years.

00:07:19.256 --> 00:07:21.153
But, I wasn't done, I
knew that I wasn't done.

00:07:21.153 --> 00:07:24.700
I went because my sister
was absolutely distraught.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:25.890
She's 50.

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:27.650
She's my stepsister, technically.

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:30.690
Her mother died when I
was 16 of breast cancer.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.780
And she, since then,

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:35.370
has taken on the role of
being like a sister mom.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.330
And, she was tracking me
everywhere I went down here.

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.130
I mean, I let her, you
know, like, safety wise but-

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:40.447
- [Mark] So, that was your
mom as well, who passed away?

00:07:40.447 --> 00:07:41.888
- It was my stepmother.

00:07:41.888 --> 00:07:42.721
- [Mark] Your stepmother.

00:07:42.721 --> 00:07:43.554
- Mm hm.

00:07:43.554 --> 00:07:44.470
A little bit background,

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:47.340
my parents got divorced when I was-

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.450
When my dad asked for a divorce

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.090
when I was like not even
like six months old.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:52.480
He was planning on leaving,

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:53.990
and then my mother got pregnant.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:56.650
And his father had done
that to his mother.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:57.980
So, he thought that it was better to stay

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:59.390
until after the birth,

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.130
and then ask for one.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.320
Which, you know, whatever.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:07.420
And so my parents didn't
think they could have kids.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:08.420
I was a bit of an accident.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:11.020
I have a brother who's
adopted from Santiago, Chile.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:16.110
My mother got pregnant
when she was younger

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:19.940
before abortions were legal.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:22.230
And she did what she had to do.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:23.063
So, because of that

00:08:23.063 --> 00:08:24.290
she thought that she
was permanently damaged

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.060
and then I came along.

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:26.893
And

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.709
so, my dad left

00:08:29.709 --> 00:08:30.620
(Maggie clears throat her)

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:33.700
and he was-

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:35.953
I'm half Mexican and there's
a term that we call mujeriego,

00:08:35.953 --> 00:08:37.220
he's a womanizer.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.430
And in his prime, he was
a bit of a womanizer.

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:41.260
So, he was cheating on my mother

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:42.350
with several different women,

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:44.130
but then he ultimately
married one of them.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.633
And that is my sister's mom.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:51.300
And she is basically, it's
like, I almost had two mothers

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:54.550
because my mom will give
you the shirt off her back.

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:55.900
She's the most caring woman,

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.780
she's a retired elementary
school principal.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.783
And she loves just anybody,
just off the bat, just because.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.890
But, as far as her independence
and strength as a woman,

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.113
that's not her forte, it's just not.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.390
And I got that.

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:16.223
I get my independence and my
strength from my stepmother.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:19.430
So, she passed away when I was 16.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:23.132
And my sister has kind of been
like a sister mom since then.

00:09:23.132 --> 00:09:23.965
So, I went to treatment

00:09:23.965 --> 00:09:28.690
because I couldn't listen to how-

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:30.100
I couldn't put her through that anymore,

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:31.000
but I wasn't done.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:38.133
And that was May of last year.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:44.080
And then I relapsed
maybe two or three months

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.450
after being sober.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.700
I was on the East Coast at this point

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.400
in a program in Baltimore.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:51.233
And

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:54.270
I just, I wasn't done.

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.810
And my parents sent me,

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.190
or I chose to come back out here

00:09:58.190 --> 00:09:59.710
to where it had some success.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.730
And that was in January of this year.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:05.570
And, I sat in the program
and knew I wasn't done.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:07.480
I still had some money in the bank

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:10.049
and I chose to come back down here.

00:10:10.049 --> 00:10:11.880
I rent at the hotel I stayed at before.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.283
And I lived down here
from January until July.

00:10:16.825 --> 00:10:17.925
And the money ran out.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:21.420
Well, as much as I-

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.560
Anyway, there's nothing.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.050
The money ran out, let's
just put it that way.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:30.050
And I went to treatment just
like I said I was going to,

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:31.600
and this was in July.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.120
And I was in treatment for a month

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:35.833
and I got about two months sober.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.590
And then about a month ago

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:42.870
I couldn't even necessarily
tell you what happened,

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.310
normally when I relapse, I see it coming.

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:46.143
I see it,

00:10:46.143 --> 00:10:47.210
I know it's going to come,

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:48.043
I understand it.

00:10:48.043 --> 00:10:49.343
I went to church on Sunday morning.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.340
A guy had hurt me really
bad the night before.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:55.350
And I felt like-

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:56.640
Like, this is a guy that
took advantage of me,

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.060
the same guy that left me

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:02.220
when I was when I was going
through that procedure

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:03.560
and I relapsed.

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.140
He claims he's got seven years sober,

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:07.120
but he stole my medication and like-

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:08.020
It's just all messy.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.350
And he and I began talking again

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.040
when I was sober this last time.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:14.080
And he told me that he didn't
really want to be with me,

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:15.830
he wanted to slow things down.

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:17.841
And this was somebody who chased after me,

00:11:17.841 --> 00:11:18.807
wanted to propose to me

00:11:18.807 --> 00:11:19.640
when I was at like my lowest point.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:20.810
And I want nothing to do with him

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:21.810
because he took such advantage.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:26.810
And I thought, "If he
doesn't even love me,

00:11:27.136 --> 00:11:29.340
if I'm not even good
enough for him to love me

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:32.457
then what's the point?"

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:35.140
I mean, he should be begging

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:37.770
for me to grace him with his presence.

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.720
I know that sounds egotistical,

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:42.500
but it just really hit.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.660
And, like I said, I went to church.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.300
I went for some wings afterwards,

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:47.820
football was on at Buffalo Wild Wings.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:52.820
I ordered a beer, one beer
later, I'm back down here.

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:55.753
And,

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:58.344
I mean,

00:11:58.344 --> 00:11:59.880
I could go right back

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.913
but that's what I always do.

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:05.080
And right now I feel like
I have to take the time to-

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:06.160
I can't do the end now,

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:08.480
I can't disappoint my
family like that anymore.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:10.060
I can't do it to myself.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.683
It's so demoralizing.

00:12:14.036 --> 00:12:15.760
The rooms of AA are
supposed to be welcoming

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:17.323
and they're not always that way.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.720
And I just have to
figure out whether or not

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:25.190
I'm ever gonna want to be sober.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:26.510
Or whether I need to figure out a way

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:28.440
to just live my life while I'm doing this,

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:29.760
because I'm just not going to put it down

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:31.043
to be honest with myself.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.950
How I started using in the first place.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:38.963
You know, there's a-

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:42.243
The suburbs can be just
as messy as the ghetto,

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:45.620
we just put on a better facade.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:50.620
At five years old my
brother began molesting me,

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:51.570
my adopted brother.

00:12:53.527 --> 00:12:58.527
And, at this point he has
so many mental issues.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:01.420
He's developmentally about
five, seven years behind

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:02.253
his actual age,

00:13:02.253 --> 00:13:04.160
which isn't that big of
a deal now that he's 40.

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:07.676
But, back then it was about 12,
you know, it was a big deal.

00:13:07.676 --> 00:13:10.380
His mother was on
everything under the sun,

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.880
at 13 years old, in Chile,
during the pregnancy

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:14.070
and gave birth.

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:17.300
And then he was in the
orphanage for like five years,

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:18.520
which is a long time.

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:20.070
I actually have a friend from high school

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.120
who was in the exact same
orphanage as my brother,

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:23.750
adopted right at birth.

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:25.340
And then my brother-

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:26.860
And it's just-

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:28.580
It's almost like how they
used to give kids opium

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:29.413
in the twenties,

00:13:29.413 --> 00:13:30.730
because they didn't know
it was bad for them.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:33.150
They didn't know how to handle
his emotional disorders.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:35.160
They didn't, and they, you know,

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.060
did the best that they could, I guess.

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:40.190
So, I was constantly getting blamed

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:41.120
for stuff that he was doing,

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:42.870
so I was terrified to say anything.

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:44.440
You know, I just thought
I would get blamed.

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:45.380
You know, my parents couldn't fathom

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:46.833
that he had this many issues.

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:49.360
And so, you know,

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:53.010
I finally said something to my mom.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:56.910
And, yeah, after he ultimately raped me,

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:58.550
he would call it tickling.

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:00.730
I've also seen my brother-

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:03.100
We went to pick him up for
his weekend with him mom

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:04.920
and he didn't want to go.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:08.100
So, he jumped out of the
car and my dad told my mom

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:09.360
I had to go wait in the house.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.020
He comes in through the back door

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:13.290
with a big butcher knife,

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:14.730
gets on top of my mom on the couch

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:16.510
and takes it to her throat.

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:18.610
And I was maybe nine years old

00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:20.870
and I just was screaming, bloody murder.

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:22.070
I've seen him attempt to kill probably

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:23.300
every member of my family.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:26.043
He's attempted to set my arm
on fire when I was three.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:28.973
And

00:14:31.287 --> 00:14:32.530
it's just, you know, that-

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:34.520
I didn't even remember the abuse

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.870
until I was in high school
at a church retreat.

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:39.340
And the first time I did heroin

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:43.860
I felt like I didn't have to
worry about anything anymore.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:45.730
I didn't have to be the
mediator between my parents

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:47.500
so that I could make sure
that I got my needs met,

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:49.760
so I can make sure that I
had money for the school trip

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:51.530
or this or that because
mom didn't want talk to dad

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:52.967
and dad want to talk to mom.

00:14:52.967 --> 00:14:57.580
And after my stepmother died when I was 16

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:59.360
my dad became very
selfish because he'd felt

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.070
that he'd been a caregiver for so long,

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:02.370
he didn't need to anymore.

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:04.670
He figured the fact that
he had a special needs son

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:06.020
and a 16 year old daughter.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.250
And so it's just-

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:13.830
I was 18 years old, I didn't even know

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:16.430
that you could do heroin any
other way than shooting it.

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:17.560
I was dating this guy

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:23.663
and I tried it and it was
an instant love affair.

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:28.510
And that, like I said, that was 18.

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:31.260
And I dealt with a lot
of those emotional issues

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:33.410
in my first couple of treatment centers.

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:34.620
And I think that's part of the reason why

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:35.720
I didn't go back to it,

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:37.070
even though I did drink
and do other stuff.

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:39.063
But, I didn't go back to
that particular substance.

00:15:40.558 --> 00:15:41.391
And

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:44.660
then the accident happened

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.650
and I just kind of thought, "Okay, God,"

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:47.920
you know, I'd, kind of, met my quota

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:49.720
for trauma for my life, you know?

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:51.060
All right, we're good,
we got it out of the way,

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:52.253
okay, fine, cool.

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:54.800
You know, I'm good to go.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:56.060
And then the accident happened.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.720
And, like I said, my pelvis was shattered.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:02.430
Now, you know, that no one
was raping me in that sense

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:04.040
it brought up a whole bunch of-

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.233
It reopened all of those wounds.

00:16:07.616 --> 00:16:09.630
And I mean, I didn't know,

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:10.590
you know, I didn't think that, you know,

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:13.180
getting run over by car
was going to, you know,

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:17.190
bring up some of the
abuse, everything again.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:19.390
I felt like I was just
being re traumatized.

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:21.403
And

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:24.360
so it's-

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:26.160
You know, I had my fair share of,

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.270
you know, like everybody does-

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:29.580
I don't like to compare.

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:30.413
Some people are like,

00:16:30.413 --> 00:16:31.310
"Oh my trauma's worse than your trauma."

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:32.250
Trauma's trauma.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:35.900
I don't know how you cope
or relate to your trauma

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:37.373
and how that affects you

00:16:37.373 --> 00:16:38.820
and you don't know how that affects me.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:40.100
Something that doesn't affect me at all

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:41.550
might devastate someone else.

00:16:42.689 --> 00:16:47.650
And I do look at my
compassion as a blessing

00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:49.200
out of what happened with my brother.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.200
Unlike your average rape
victim or rape abuser

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.960
where you can just X them out of your life

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:55.930
and everybody hates them.

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:57.827
And, you know, Dad comes
to the rescue and like,

00:16:57.827 --> 00:17:00.130
you know, how dare they.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:03.180
You can't get rid of them, you know?

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:07.397
And you have to figure
out a way to cohabitate.

00:17:07.397 --> 00:17:09.800
And today he's gay.

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:13.190
And, you know, it's hard
for him to even admit

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:14.023
what happened.

00:17:14.023 --> 00:17:14.856
He has.

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:18.170
He has his own struggles with alcohol

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:23.410
and we have a very minimal relationship.

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:24.800
And I'm able to set boundaries

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:28.580
and I just really don't speak
to him for the most part.

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.630
- [Mark] Who do you speak
to still in your family?

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:32.743
- My sister, my mother.

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:36.333
I don't speak to my
father while I'm using.

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:40.520
I barely talked to my
father in the last year.

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:42.270
I was supposed to meet
him at a baseball game

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:44.120
last labor day,

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:45.610
and instead I got high.

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:46.443
And-

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:49.600
Yeah.

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:50.433
- [Mark] Is he out here?

00:17:50.433 --> 00:17:51.490
- No, my family is all back-

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:53.040
Well, my sister's in Seattle

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:55.040
and the rest of my family's in Virginia.

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:57.420
- [Mark] So you were back home?

00:17:57.420 --> 00:17:58.253
- Mm hm.

00:17:58.253 --> 00:17:59.086
- [Mark] Oh, I see.

00:17:59.086 --> 00:18:00.243
- Yeah, this is when I was in Baltimore.

00:18:00.243 --> 00:18:01.743
I had, like, those few months.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.900
And I mean, they just, like,

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:09.346
my sister says that I'm a drug addict,

00:18:09.346 --> 00:18:11.410
but I'm a good person.

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:13.120
And, she's like, "That's
what makes it so difficult.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:14.670
If you're a horrible person, like,

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:15.582
it would be so easy to hate you."

00:18:15.582 --> 00:18:16.415
You know?

00:18:16.415 --> 00:18:18.517
"Or to be bad at what you're doing."

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:22.853
And I don't even-

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:27.763
I don't necessarily use
today because I hate myself,

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:29.483
I don't.

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:33.510
I'm not entirely certain why I use.

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:38.510
I don't know, I mean, I've
been to 16 treatment centers

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:42.230
that doesn't include
detoxes and outpatients.

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:47.582
And, you know, I really thought this time,

00:18:47.582 --> 00:18:49.060
like, I was in a great space.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:51.260
I just didn't see this coming.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:52.093
I didn't.

00:18:52.093 --> 00:18:53.870
And that's what, I guess,

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:56.833
has gotten me so thrown
for a loop right now.

00:18:59.852 --> 00:19:03.570
- [Mark] What do you think
the solution might be

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:05.063
to get clean for you?

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:07.183
If it isn't,

00:19:07.183 --> 00:19:10.224
then you just hate yourself
like a lot of people here do.

00:19:10.224 --> 00:19:11.550
Though, their standards are so low

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:14.660
that this environment and drug abuse

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:15.570
and all that works for them

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:19.140
'cause it's where they
subconsciously grasp.

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:21.470
But, for you, you know, you were a dancer,

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:23.623
you were very accomplished.

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:27.700
So, for you it almost seems
like a very different animal.

00:19:28.835 --> 00:19:31.890
(Maggie sighs)

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:33.800
- People give me a start here.

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:37.570
People view me as a sucker too,

00:19:37.570 --> 00:19:39.333
but people-

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:44.110
I feel like, you know, I try
to do the next right thing.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:46.500
And like, maybe if I wasn't here,

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:48.750
like, there was a woman,
she's my brother's age,

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:52.260
and she got robbed of her bag
of clothes and everything.

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:54.400
And, this was back in like March.

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:55.740
She was freezing outside of the bank

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:56.902
and she never asked me for drugs,

00:19:56.902 --> 00:19:58.075
she never asked me for money.

00:19:58.075 --> 00:19:59.367
I don't think she'd ask me for cigarettes.

00:19:59.367 --> 00:20:01.107
And, you know, I saw her
one night and, you know,

00:20:01.107 --> 00:20:02.390
and I went to my hotel room

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:04.650
and got her blankets and everything like,

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:06.020
who would have done that?

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:07.910
And I kind of feel good about myself.

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:10.680
My struggle is when I get clean,

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:14.020
they want us to sit in programs

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.430
for like the first year,
"Don't do anything,

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:17.380
don't get involved"-

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:18.580
I can't do that.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:20.990
I've been such a waste
of space for so long

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:23.450
that I feel like they
really need to look at more

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:25.220
of how they do it in like Portugal,

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:26.610
where they give people a purpose.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:28.077
The opposite of addiction is connection.

00:20:28.077 --> 00:20:29.550
- [Mark] Yeah, that's what I was going to-

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:32.720
- Mm hm, and the other
part of it's purpose.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:35.850
You know, addicts and alcoholics
are such passionate people.

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:40.222
And we, you know, we've got
so much to offer, we feel.

00:20:40.222 --> 00:20:42.770
And we care so much that what do we do

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:43.700
with all this extra energy

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:44.533
that maybe other people are like,

00:20:44.533 --> 00:20:45.940
"Why do you even give a crap?"

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:46.773
You know?

00:20:47.616 --> 00:20:48.650
Or, how does that affect you?

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:51.172
Like, I don't even care,
don't let it affect you.

00:20:51.172 --> 00:20:54.634
And, my thing is like,
I got to get back in

00:20:54.634 --> 00:20:56.490
and doing something.

00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:58.700
But, instead it's like you've
got the insurance systems

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:00.540
where they want to keep you in treatment

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:01.373
because they're making money.

00:21:01.373 --> 00:21:04.370
You've got treatment centers
that are paying you to go.

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:07.050
They've got treatment centers
where people are coming in

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:08.002
that work for the treatment center,

00:21:08.002 --> 00:21:09.090
got hired by the treatment centers

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:10.300
to convinced you to relapse

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:12.230
so that you have to go back
to the treatment center

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:13.450
and then all over again

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.330
and get all of the insurance money.

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:16.180
- [Mark] I'm listening to your story

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:18.520
and certainly you've been through a lot.

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:23.090
So there's that, but you
also seem to have the resolve

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:25.990
and where with all to
do great things in life,

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:27.480
which a lot of these
people I've spoken to,

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:30.770
kind of, just don't for
a multitude of reasons.

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:33.920
And, it almost seems
like what might save you

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:38.593
is a great relationship with somebody.

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:41.083
You know?

00:21:41.083 --> 00:21:42.039
- Yeah.

00:21:42.039 --> 00:21:43.990
- [Mark] I don't know what
the solution is to that.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:45.890
I don't know where you
go about finding that.

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:48.156
But, miracles like that do happen.

00:21:48.156 --> 00:21:50.564
- They do.

00:21:50.564 --> 00:21:51.397
- [Mark] 'Cause the thing is, like,

00:21:51.397 --> 00:21:52.490
going to another treatment,

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:54.443
same thing, that's not going to do it.

00:21:57.690 --> 00:22:01.393
Finding something to replace
your creative outlet-

00:22:02.332 --> 00:22:03.165
- I mean I've got ideas.

00:22:03.165 --> 00:22:05.430
- [Mark] That might be helpful,

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:10.117
but then in combination
with someone in your life

00:22:10.117 --> 00:22:11.770
could be good.

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:13.430
- I would have to agree with that.

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:16.220
You know, because I do stay sober, like,

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:17.860
when I was with the guy for, you know,

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:19.207
I had a year and a half.

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:25.500
And then

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:28.180
when things like that
fall apart, you know?

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:29.143
- [Mark] Yeah, which they do.

00:22:29.143 --> 00:22:30.010
- Which they do.

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:31.330
And it's like how-

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:32.920
I also tend, in relationships,

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:35.830
do a lot of work on my romantic,

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:36.880
well, just in general relationships.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:39.440
And one of my character defects is that

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:41.930
I will give and give and give of myself

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:45.300
and then expect the other
person to do the same,

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:46.350
and they don't.

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:47.877
And I sacrifice my own
needs because I'm like,

00:22:47.877 --> 00:22:49.780
"Okay, well, it's my turn to sacrifice.

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:51.050
I'm going to need them at some point."

00:22:51.050 --> 00:22:52.360
And, you know,

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:54.090
this is just what you do in relationships.

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:56.000
And then time after time,

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:00.200
I'm not met with the same
love and care and concern

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:02.163
and acceptance that I met them with.

00:23:05.020 --> 00:23:08.060
And that happens in a
lot of my relationships.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:10.210
You know, not just
romantic but friendship.

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:13.440
And so, you know, that's
something I have to look at

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:15.340
because I didn't realize

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:17.730
that I have unrealistic
expectations of people.

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:20.298
- [Mark] Yeah, well that's
on you to figure out.

00:23:20.298 --> 00:23:21.390
- Right.

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:22.984
- [Mark] You can point
the finger on other people

00:23:22.984 --> 00:23:23.817
all day long.

00:23:23.817 --> 00:23:24.720
- Right.

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:27.169
- [Mark] But, that's ultimately
something you're doing.

00:23:27.169 --> 00:23:28.002
- Mm hm.

00:23:30.742 --> 00:23:32.130
- [Mark] Well, thank you so
much for telling me your story.

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:33.599
- Thank you.

00:23:33.599 --> 00:23:35.095
- [Mark] Very moving.

00:23:35.095 --> 00:23:36.880
And you're very different from
everyone I've spoken to here.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:38.320
You don't look the part

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:42.270
and you also clearly aren't
your typical skid row person.

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:43.507
- I get that, yeah.

00:23:43.507 --> 00:23:45.440
- [Mark] And good luck,

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:46.460
thank you for being patient.

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:47.293
- Yep.

